.SH "Constructor & Destructor Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::Model (\fBModelTopology\fP & mesh_topology, \fBVSet\fP< dim > & vset, const char * var_file, bool treat_domains_as_regions_and_use_regions_file)"

.PP
constructs model with subdomains (\fBRegion\fP, \fBBoundary\fP, \fBSplitBoundary\fP), variables file name with extension "*-variables\&.txt" where * is the name of the model Constructor is similar to previous one, but only a subset of the model geometry stored in the vset will be used according to the specifications given by the data of the model topology object\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIvset\fP A \fBVSet\fP is supplied as constructor argument and should contain a finite-element mesh and associated properties with names which must correspond to the properties specified in the Property input file (default: CSMP_variables\&.txt)\&.
.br
\fImesh_topology\fP a \fBModelTopology\fP object which should be initialized as is done for instance by methods of the \fBANSYS_Interface\fP\&. The model topology object has a rich interface which allows the user to select sub portions of the model stored in the vset\&.
.br
\fItreat_domains_as_regions_and_use_regions_file\fP when there is a "-regions\&.txt" file, any elements of regions that are not mentioned in there will be eliminated from the mesh when the CSMP models gets build\&. This is important because meshing tools like ANSYS mesh every part of the BREP using lines, surfaces and volumes\&. However intervening lines or surfaces are normally not desired because element integral contributions would be duplicated for elements which share the same nodes\&. This is also why \fBANSYS_Interface\fP eliminates the lower-dimensional elements from regions if all these element groups have the same name\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The constructor first initializes the \fBMeshManager\fP and then the MemoryManager with the data from the \fBVSet\fP\&. Then property data stored in the \fBVSet\fP as \fBFEM_Data\fP are used to initialize some of the properties for which memory was allocated for by the MemoryManager\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Constructor will build a \fBModel\fP for 2D meshes from 'Triangle' as well as 3D meshes from GoCad or NASTRAN input data\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
If no properties are supplied, the constructor will issue a Warning message\&. In this case, property values have to be assigned before a computation is carried out since all basic variables are initialized to NAN (not a number) by default\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
per default this constructor will not create any boundaries

.PP
the regions file is used only if there is one 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::Model (\fBVSet\fP< dim > & vset, const char * var_file)"

.PP
using the supplied polygonal data constructs unnamed single-domain model without regions or boundaries Builds 2 and 3D, finite-element meshes according to the element specifications given in the supplied \fBVSet\fP\&. The vset will contain elements of different types, for instance, tetrahedra and prism elements\&. If, these are not already stored in the \fBVSet\fP, different material properties can be assigned to regions inside the the calculation\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIvset\fP A \fBVSet\fP is supplied as constructor argument and should contain a finite-element mesh and associated properties with names which must correspond to the properties specified in the Property input file (default: CSMP_variables\&.txt)\&. Note that \fBVSet\fP is not const because it may be shrunk in construction process)\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Constructor is used when an ANSYS \fBModel\fP is built from topology and \fBVData\fP\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
No boundaries or regions can be created here because there is no way to store corresponding information in the \fBVSet\fP\&. Use a constructor with \fBModel\fP topology to achieve this\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::Model (\fBVSet\fP< dim > & vset)\fR [explicit]\fP"

.PP
constructs purely topological unnamed single-domain model without regions, boundaries nor variable storage Build model without variable storage\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
No boundaries or regions are created either because there is no way to store corresponding information in the \fBVSet\fP\&. Use a constructor with \fBModel\fP topology to achieve this\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::~\fBModel\fP ()\fR [virtual]\fP"

.PP
destructor that needs to be overloaded when a subclass is derived from model removes dynamically allocated finite volume stencil manager 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::Model ()\fR [protected]\fP"

.PP
default constructor that is custom-made Constructs a completely empty model\&. It is used by one of the ANSYS \fBModel\fP interfaces\&.

.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
model will contain a single region called 'model' and it will be considered unique\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
SKM deprecate; make the default constructor private to comply with inheritance principles 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::Model (const char * varTextFile)\fR [explicit]\fP, \fR [protected]\fP"

.PP
to construct model as a base class to a derived model built from an external dataset using a variable file in ASCII format Constructor used for base class construction in subclasses of the model\&. \fBExample\fP: \fBANSYS_Model3D\fP where model construction input data are read from text file, initializing \fBVSet\fP and \fBModelTopology\fP classes that serve as an input for model construction\&.

.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
this is not a stand-alone constructor and leaves the model in an incomplete state\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SH "Member Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::Accept (\fBcsmp::Visitor\fP< dim > & v)"

.PP
support of the visitor pattern giving visitors access to the model The User's guide describes the \fBVisitor\fP design pattern which was adopted from Gamma et al\&. 1994 (Design Patterns, Addison Wesley, p\&. 331)\&. Depending on its internally specified ApplicationLevel, \fBAccept()\fP either applies the \fBVisitor\fP to the entire \fBModel\fP or passes it down to members of the \fBModel\fP hierarchy (\fBRegion\fP > > \fBBoundary\fP > \fBElement\fP > \fBFace\fP > \fBInterFace\fP > \fBNode\fP\&. In the last case, the visitor is passed to all nodes of the entire \fBModel\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIv\fP Any visitor derived from the \fBVisitor\fP base class\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The method consists a of switch( visitor\&.ApplicationLevel() ) statement with iterator loops through the different object collections\&. These deliver the visitor to its ApplicationTarget()\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Standard method to give visitors access to the \fBModel\fP\&. 
.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
visitors will only be applied to the unique regions in the model in order to avoid duplicate application\&. If a non-unique region is the target, the visitor should be passed directly to it\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Messages"
.PP
\fBAccept()\fP will report an error if the visitors destination is a specific region, which is not defined\&.

.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
SKM (1) Reorganize this so that no confusion remains where the visitor goes\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::Apply (\fBInterrelation\fP< dim > & relation, const char * region = \fR'Model< dim >'\fP)"

.PP
prompts the region-by-region, element-by-element or node-by-node calculation of the target variable via the interrelation Applies a calculation recipe which is specified in an \fBInterrelation\fP subclass to every element/face/node etc\&. in the current \fBModel\fP\&. If the \fBInterrelation\fP applies to a single \fBRegion\fP (subregion of the \fBModel\fP) it is passed on to the interface of the corresponding \fBRegion\fP object\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIrelation\fP A user-defined subclass of an \fBInterrelation\fP base class\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
Executes the method \fBApply()\fP on interrelation objects derived from the \fBInterrelation\fP base class\&. \fBApply()\fP will in turn call Calculate() in the class derived from the \fBInterrelation\fP base class (subclass)\&. Remember that Interrelations are defined as calculations that do not require the inversion of a global matrix\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Examples of interrelations include the calculation of strains from displacements, the horizontal and vertical components of the fluid-flow velocity from nodal pressures and/or elemental permeabilities and porosities\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
Please consult the documentation of the \fBInterrelation\fP class and the derived subclasses for a description of the output which results when these objects are applied to the \fBModel\fP or a \fBRegion\fP\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::Apply (\fBPDE_Integrator\fP< dim, \fBElement\fP > & problem, bool debug = \fRfalse\fP)"

.PP
application of integration scheme to model, subregions thereof or boundary or split-boundary objects An Algorithm object is passed either directly to the \fBModel\fP or to a subdomain of it to which it applies\&. In this process, the Algorithm is provided with iterators to the target collection of (default = Elements) to accumulate data, assign boundary conditions and to carry out a global matrix inversion using the '\fBSolver\fP' or any other calculation tool\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIproblem\fP An initialized Algorithm object, i\&.e\&. an algorithm to which partial differential operators have been assigned (see MathOperator\&.\&.\&. objects)\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The \fBApply()\fP method calls on the interface of the Algorithm object to carry out a sequence of operations which will build, initialize, and invert a global solution matrix in order to find distributed values of the dependent \fBvariable(s)\fP of interest\&. In detail, this sequence will vary, dependent on whether the calculation establishes transient or steady-state variable values\&. The more complicated transient calculation will call (see interface documentation of the Algorithm class):

.PP
.PP
.nf
EstablishMatrixSetup()       to allocate the solution matrix etc\&.
Accumulate()                 to assemble the solution matrix\&.
AssignInitialConditions()    to put the present model state into the
righthand vector
LateAccumulate()             to add source or sink rates into the
righthandside
AssignEssentialConditions()  assign Neumann or Dirichlet conditions
Solve()                      invert the solution matrix with the Solver
OutputResults()              test and map the solution vector back into
the Model / MemoryManager storage
PostProcess()                calculate further variable values on the
basis of the calculation results
.fi
.PP
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Standard interface for 2 and 3D global computations\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
The interface methods of the Algorithm class Accumulate() and Solve() will report the progress of a computation in a fashion that depends on the type of problem which is solved and on the solution technique\&. Please refer to the documentation of the Algorithm interface and the \fBSolver\fP to learn more about this specific output\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::Apply (\fBPDE_Integrator\fP< dim, \fBElement\fP > & problem, const char * region_name, bool debug = \fRfalse\fP)"

.PP
application of integration scheme to a particular region, boundary of split-boundary identified by name specific regions 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::AssignCellCharacteristicsTo (const char * characteristic, const char * var)"

.PP
'characteristic' options are: volume, inner radius, and aspect ratio \fBAssignCellCharacteristicsTo()\fP allows to assign a number of \fBElement\fP characteristics as identified by strings (second argument) to scalar physical variables\&. These characteristics are:

.PP
"volume" (3D)
.br
 "inner radius"
.br
 "aspect ratio" (longest / shortest segment)

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIcharacteristic\fP The element characteristic is assigned to 
.br
\fIvar\fP a property of the user's choice and as identified by its name in the variable database\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The method was implemented mainly for two-dimensional calculations\&. Therefore, height and width only work in two-dimensional calculations\&. The characteristics are obtained from the \fBFiniteElement\fP which is bridged to the current \fBElement\fP that is queried\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
The characteristic "inner radius" is commonly used to find the appropriate resolution for an advection or visualization grid on which a variable is to be mapped on\&.

.PP
\fBAssignCellCharacteristicsTo()\fP can be used to:
.IP "\(bu" 2
test the shape of elements in a mesh for their suitability for a computation (aspect ratio)\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
testing how skewed the elements got by deformation
.IP "\(bu" 2
integrate element properties in specific calculations, for instance, if a fracture is just one element wide, the minimum height of these fracture elements may be equivalent to the fracture aperture\&.
.PP
.SH "Messages"
.PP
Firstly, the method will report an error and return, if the target property to which the element characteristic shall be assigned to is not an element variable\&.

.PP
Further errors may be reported if the user tries to use the characteristic area in a 3D computation, or volume in a 2D computation\&. Also, element height and width are thus far only available in 2D\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::AssignNodeCoordinatesTo (const char * vector_variable)"

.PP
transfers node coordinates to target \fBVectorVariable<dim>\fP Assigns node coordinates to the supplied node variable which may be either of scalar or vector type\&. If either the X, Y, or Z coordinate are to be assigned to a scalar variable an index from 0\&.\&.\&.dim-1 must be supplied to indicate which coordinate axis shall be mapped\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIvector_variable\fP The name of the physical variable to which the node coordinate shall be assigned to, and, if a scalar property shall be initialized with coordinate values, an int index to indicate which coordinate axis is desired (0=X, 1=Y, 2=Z)\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
Method uses the Coordinates() and X(), Y(), and Z() interfaces of the \fBNode\fP class\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
To arrive at coordinate values for a model where topography is important etc\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
The method will report a fatal error and terminate the computation, if the target variable is not placed on the node or if it is not of the correct type\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::ChangePropertyStatus (const char * input_prop, \fBVARIABLE_FLAG\fP new_status)"

.PP
changes the flags of the target variable all across the model The status of the distributed physical variable as identified by the string char*, is changed from the first \fBVARIABLE_FLAG\fP argument to the second\&. This is done only where the distributed physical variable has a status equivalent to that given by the first \fBVARIABLE_FLAG\fP argument of this method\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIinput_prop\fP Name of property the status of which shall be changed at where it is flagged equivalent to argument two\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
To drop Dirichlet boundary conditions from a previous timestep etc\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
If no assignments can be made by ChangeConditionFromTo(), because no object properties with the desired flags are found, an error is reported\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::ChangePropertyStatusWhere (const char * var, double vmin, double vmax, \fBVARIABLE_FLAG\fP new_status)"

.PP
where the values of the target property are within the given range the flag of the target variables are changed to the new status Where the specified property has a value within the range given by min-max, its status is changed\&.

.PP
The status of the distributed physical variable as identified by the string char*, is changed to the status defined by the \fBVARIABLE_FLAG\fP argument of this method, if the value of the distributed variable lies within the range given as open interval by the fTs min and max\&. This method only works for scalar and vector variables\&. The status of vector variables is changed only if all elements of the \fBVectorVariable<dim>\fP are within the defined value range\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Data in the specified range may be preserved if set to a DIRICH value or one may choose to form a group on the basis of their status or by intersecting a \fBRegion\fP with such a group\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
Thus far this method cannot change the status of \fBTensorVariable<dim>\fP's and will therefore issue a warning if this is attempted\&.

.PP
Also if no status changes were made, the method will report this\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> bool \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::CopyGradientOfProperty_A_To_B (const char * prop_a, const char * prop_b)"

.PP
computes the (constant valued) first spatial derivative of the node property and assigns it to the element property / barycenter of the element Provided that property 'a' is a scalar node variable and property 'b' is a vector variable placed on the element, \fBCopyGradientOfProperty_A_To_B()\fP will calculate the gradient of property 'a' for each element and assign the result to the element variable 'b'\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIprop_a\fP specifies the scalar node variable of which the gradient will be calculated second string identifies the vector variable placed on the element which will store the calculated gradient of property 'a'\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
The definition of many geophysical or geochemical interrelations requires a knowledge of property gradients which can be calculated with this method, using the element interpolation functions of type of finite element which was used to build the mesh\&. The method can also be used for post-processing following the application of algorithms\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
\fBCopyGradientOfProperty_A_To_B()\fP will return without completing any calculations, if the input variables do not comply with the specifications outlined above\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::CopyReplace (const char * from, const char * to)"

.PP
replaces the values of the target propery (to) with the values of property (from); both variables must have the same type and placement \fBCopyReplace()\fP replaces the values of the variable 'to' with the values of the variable 'from'\&. Note, that this operation can only be defined for variables which have the same type and the same placement\&. Note also, that variable flags such as DIRICH are also copied to the target variable\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIfrom\fP The variable names of the physical variable (property) which is copied 
.br
\fIto\fP the variable name that will be replaced\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Use \fBCopyReplace()\fP, for instance to make backups of variable values before applying time-dependent calculations or to calculate time derivatives (via self-defined interrelations) from the second before last, the last and the present variable value\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
The method will return without modifying any variable values if the type or the placement of the input variables are not the same\&. In this case an error will be reported\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBcsmp::Index\fP \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::CreateProperty (const char * new_prop, const char * notation, const char * unit, \fBVARIABLE_TYPE\fP type = \fRSCALAR\fP, \fBPLACEMENT\fP place = \fRNODE\fP, uint32_t vsize = \fR1\fP, double vmin = \fR\-1\&.0e+30\fP, double vmax = \fR1\&.0e+30\fP, std::string usage = \fR'???'\fP)"

.PP
inserts (if new) variable into the database and creates storage for it on the entities where it shall be discretized Add a property at runtime 
.PP
\fBWarning\fP
.RS 4
Any runtime change in variables invalidates existing \fBIndex\fP objects! They need to be refreshed\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
(1-F) Support for BOUNDARY\&.\&.\&. / SPLIT_BOUNDARY (create overload taking index) 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBPropertyDatabase\fP< dim > & \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::Database ()"

.PP
returns reference to database object where all variable access and type specifications are stored \fBDatabase()\fP comes in a constant and a volatile version, giving you access to the \fBPropertyDatabase\fP object inside the \fBModel\fP\&.

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Either a constant or a volatile reference to the \fBPropertyDatabase\fP object\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::DeleteProperty (const char * property)"

.PP
deletes property from the database and the distributed containers all across the model Removes a property from the model\&. 
.PP
\fBWarning\fP
.RS 4
All indices have to be updated\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
(1-F) Support for new placements 
.RE
.PP
\fBWarning\fP
.RS 4
Existing \fBIndex\fP objects may be invalidated after invoking this function 
.RE
