.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::ExtrapolateCellToNodeProperty (const char * eprop, const char * nprop, bool by_distance = \fRtrue\fP)"

.PP
piecewise constant element property values are extrapolated to nodes using a choice of averaging schemes (1/distance vs\&. element-volume weighted) In a finite-element mesh, each node is shared by a variable number of elements which also may differ in their area\&. This method extrapolates element properties to node properties by calculating the element property average of all elements which share the node, weighted by element area for each node of the mesh\&.

.PP
'by_distance' means that weighting is not by the area/volume of the neighbouring element but by the distance of its barycenter to the node to which the extrapolation is done\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIeprop\fP The name of the element property which is extrapolated, 
.br
\fInprop\fP the name of the node property to which the extrapolation is written to\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The element property values are weighted by the area of the element and then added for each node to a property array vector, the number of additions to each node is counted\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
To obtain higher order (more continuous) velocity fields, the TransportVisitor object uses the element property 'velocity' and the 'nodal velocity' at the same time\&. Other applications include interrelations where the output property is a node variable, but which require element properties in the calculations\&.

.PP
While the extrapolation approach used in this method is topologically sound, the method may still produce somewhat patchy looking results in meshes where the element size varies greatly and a node lies on the boundary of 2 regions with strongly differing material properties\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
The method checks the placement of the specified variables and reports an error if it does not match the specifications from above\&. In this case it will return without carrying out the extrapolation\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::ExtrapolateIntegrationPointToNodeProperty (const char * cprop, const char * nprop)"

.PP
linearly extrapolates the values of the integration point variable to the element nodes where an averaging with the neighbor elements is performed Using the element interpolation functions, this method extrapolates the desired constraint point property to the nodes\&. Constributions of adjacent elements are averaged but no weighting by element size or proximity of barycentre to the node is applied\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIcprop\fP The names of the targeted constraint point 
.br
\fInprop\fP and node variables\&.
.RE
.PP
The result of the extrapolation is returned into the \fBModel\fP property storage\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The method depends on a corresponding function of the \fBFiniteElement\fP which is not implemented for all finite element types but for all isoparametric elements\&. In quadratic isoparametric elements the method uses a linear extrapolation which is justified for stresses or strains, for instance, because they define a trilinear field on the IntegrationPoints\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
A consistency check on variable type and placement is performed\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::IndexByPropertyValues ()"

.PP
renumbers everything, using stored "node number" etc\&. if available, else \fBMeshManager\fP is prompted to created unique numbering Sets the Idx values of the nodes, elements, faces, and interfaces of the model according to the stored values, if any\&. 
.PP
.nf
"node number", "element number", "face number", "interface number"

.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
if elements were created or deleted, then there may now be gaps in the numbering\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::Initialize (const char * regions_file_prefix, \fBModelTopology\fP & mesh_topology, \fBVSet\fP< dim > & vset)\fR [protected]\fP"

.PP
builds model treating any topologic entities from regions file as unique regions; later converts correspondingly labelled ones into boundaries and split boundaries must not contain any \fBFace\fP or \fBInterFace\fP objects which will be built by this method

.PP
Initialise( topology, vset, create boundary \&.\&.\&.)

.PP
Performs the following steps:

.PP
.IP "0." 4
Using the regions file, selects wanted mesh regions topology and corresponding elements from vset
.IP "1." 4
Reduces mesh size and element types to those left over in the topology object
.IP "2." 4
initializing the finite-element manager
.IP "3." 4
builds finite element mesh and property storage -> done by \fBMeshManager\fP
.IP "4." 4
forms unique \fBRegion\fP called \fBModel\fP (is in unique regions if there are no other unique regions) else in non-unique Regions
.IP "5." 4
Tests with a flood-fill whether the model is contiguous
.IP "6." 4
Assigns node and element material properties and variable values to model -> done by InputVariablesFrom(vset)
.IP "7." 4
Associates supplied subregions with regions (model subdomains) -> done by FormRegionsFrom(topology)
.IP "8." 4
Forms Boundaries -> done by EstablishBoundaries() or \fBEstablishBoxBoundaries()\fP if the model is box-shaped
.IP "9." 4
Adds required property storage for regions and boundaries (however their properties are not initialised here)
.PP

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
MOST COMMONLY USED MODEL CONSTRUCTION METHOD FOR INPUT FROM ANSYS via the CSP interface

.PP
a fully valid \fBVSet\fP is expected by this method\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
should only be used for models created externally\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIregions_file_prefix\fP use to select regions in ANSYS or other mesh  
.br
\fImesh_topology\fP stores tbe model name as well as the regions in the form of element idx 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::Initialize (\fBModelTopology\fP & mesh_topology, \fBVSet\fP< dim > & vset)\fR [protected]\fP"

.PP
builds model with regions, boundaries, and splitboundaries as identified by "BOX_BOUNDARY", "boundary" or "splitboundary" strings in the domain names must store corresponding \fBElement\fP, \fBFace\fP and \fBInterFace\fP objects matching the idx integers stored in topology

.PP
NEW (2022)! - builds model assuming that all information about regions, boundaries or split boundaries is stored in \fBModelTopology\fP\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
if there are domains in the \fBVSet\fP which consist of elements of diffierent spatial dimensions, only those of the highest one are kept\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
If the \fBModel\fP topology object enlists a \fBBoundary\fP object, the corresponding stored idx values are interpreted as \fBFace\fP ids, noting that the all entries in the \fBVSet\fP are numbered consecutively and continuously starting with \fBElement\fP followed by \fBFace\fP and \fBInterFace\fP objects\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fImesh_topology\fP must store \fBRegion\fP, \fBBoundary\fP, and \fBSplitBoundary\fP objects 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::Initialize (\fBVSet\fP< dim > & vset)\fR [protected]\fP"

.PP
builds with model without specifically named subdomains other than "Model"\&. If model is box-shaped corresponding boundary flags will exist\&. Initialises model from \fBVSet\fP\&. Similar to Initialise(VSet,ModelTopology), but without the creation of regions other than '\fBModel\fP'\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
a fully (boundary) flagged valid \fBVSet\fP is expected by this method\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> template<class T> template void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::InputBoundaryValue< \fBVectorVariable\fP< 3U > > (\fBBOX_BOUNDARY\fP , const char * input_prop, const T & value)"

.PP
sets the value of the property on those sites (nodes, elements) whose AtBoundary() function matches the \fBBOX_BOUNDARY\fP value Given a box-shaped model, ie\&. a model whose boundaries are rectangular, this method assigns uniform variable values to each of these specific boundaries including edges and model corner points\&.

.PP
Assignments to individual edges or corners are also supported\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
elements are considered boundary elements if at least one of their faces is on this boundary\&.

.PP
each side boundary shares edges and corner points with the adjacent one\&. Therefore methods like 'belongsToSide" must be used to capture all the nodes that belong to an individual boundary\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBTest\fP
.RS 4
updated by SKM 29/5/14\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::InputFromBinaryFile (const char * model_name, const std::set< std::string > & subset_variables)"

.PP
reads model written by \fBOutputToBinaryFile()\fP including all associated properties; if subset of variables is not empty only these will be read Reads model written by \fBOutputToBinaryFile()\fP including all associated properties or a subset of variables

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fImodel_name\fP of the saved model that is to be retrieved from the binary file 
.br
\fIsubset_variables\fP selected variables that shall be read from the binary file; if empty, all variables are read
.RE
.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
If the variable subset is empty and variables are encoutered in the \fBVSet\fP which are not yet contained in the current database, extra storage is created for these\&.

.PP
if any of the variables in the subset is not known to the database an error is reported\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> template<class T> template void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::InputPropertyValue< \fBFlaggedArrayVariable\fP > (const char * input_prop, const T & value)"

.PP
sets the values of the distributed variable all across the model; to enter scalar value use makeScalar(flag,value) helper function Allows assignment of uniform property values for all CSMP variable types\&. Any previous values are overwritten irrespective of their flags and will receive the flag of the input variable\&. It follows that potential boundary conditions need to be reassigned after this method has been called\&.

.PP
Note that the method checks the input variables against the ranges specified in the variables database (see '*-variables\&.txt' file)\&. If the values fall out of these ranges, the method will reset values to the nearest range bound\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIinput_prop\fP The name of the input property (which is checked against the property database), 
.br
\fIvalue\fP and the initialized CSP basic variable of scalar, vector, or tensor type\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Messages"
.PP
If the variable name is not defined or the variable value falls outside of the range that was specified in the current '-variables\&.txt' file the method will report errors\&.

.PP
Similarly when the property placement (i\&.e\&. element, node etc\&.) is not recognized, an error will be reported\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> template<class Var> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::InputVariableFrom (const char * input_prop, const \fBFEM_Data\fP< Var > & vdata)"
\fBInputVariableFrom()\fP lets you input variable data stored in a \fBFEM_Data\fP template class object to a \fBModel\fP variable\&. class T here is a place holder for the data type which may be a double or any basic CSP variable\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIinput_prop\fP the name of the variable to which the data are to be assigned\&. 
.br
\fIvdata\fP is a reference to the \fBFEM_Data\fP data object\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
How much do I await the day when member templates defined outside of class declarations will be mastered by the gang of C++ compilers\&. Since this is not currently the case you have to bear with overloaded method cluttered class interfaces\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
\fBFEM_Data\fP class objects are convinient containers for CSP property data and are used also by peripheral programs such as the OpenGL viewing tool\&. They can also be used to compare values of different properties of the same type and placement using STL algorithms\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
\fBInputVariableFrom()\fP will report an error and return without completing its task, if the variable type or placement in the Property database does not match the specifications of the \fBFEM_Data\fP dataset\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
all elements and nodes need to be numbered uniquely and in the same fashion as they were output to file for this method to work correctly\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::InputVariablesFrom (const \fBVSet\fP< dim > & vset)"

