.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::MinMaxCoordinates (\fBPoint\fP< dim > & xyz_min, \fBPoint\fP< dim > & xyz_max) const"

.PP
returns the opposite corners of the bounding box that encloses the model \fBMinMaxCoordinates()\fP returns the coordinate extrema of a supplied list of either \fBNode\fP, IntegrationPoint, or \fBElement\fP objects into two supplied \fBVectorVariable<dim>\fPs\&. This is equivalent to finding a bounding box which contains the enlisted objects given that they all lie in the same plane\&. If this is not the case, the results refer to a rectangular bounding box\&.

.PP
The two \fBVectorVariable<dim>\fP arguments will store the mininum and maximum coordinates of the bounding rectangle (2D) or bounding box (3D) which is defined by the object coordinates\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
Depending on the specified placement, the method loops over the \fBNode\fP, IntegrationPoint, or \fBElement\fP objects to find their coordinate extrema\&. In the case of \fBElement\fP objects, the method uses the BaryCentre (centre of gravity) of the elements as coordinate point\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
Method is used by AssignBoundaryValues()\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
\fBMinMaxCoordinates()\fP will report an error if the placement was input incorrectly\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::MinMaxOf (const char * prop, double & vmin, double & vmax) const"

.PP
returns the value range of the target property within the entire model Finds the minimum and maximum values of a physical variable which is distributed over the current finite-element mesh\&. If the property is a vector variable, the vector length, and if it is a tensor variable, the tensors determinant will be reported\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIprop\fP The name of the physical variable
.RE
.PP
The results of the range search will be returned into the second and third method arguments\&. If the variable has not been initialized, the value NAN (not a number) is returned\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
For example, \fBMinMaxOf()\fP is useful to find maximum property values which do not depend on Dirichlet boundary conditions but on applied sources and sinks\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
\fBMinMaxOf()\fP will report an error if the property of interest is unknown to the \fBPropertyDatabase\fP\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::MoveNodeCoordinatesBy (const char * vector_variable)"

.PP
shifts the node coordinates by an amount that is determined by the value of the target \fBVectorVariable<dim>\fP Displaces the node coordinates by displacements supplied as through the VECTOR variable (dim = dimensions of the model)\&. For each element, the nodes of which were displaced, the private boolean variable shape_to_date is set to false such that its volume is newly calculated once it is requested\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIvector_variable\fP The name of the nodal vector variable which holds the node coordinate displacement\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
After the assignment of the nodal displacements, \fBMoveNodeCoordinatesBy()\fP will set the \fBElement\fP state variable 'shape_to_data' to false\&. This will prompt re-calculation of elemental volumes if these are queried in subsequent computations\&.
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
If a mesh shall be deformed using the diplacements of a deformation calculation stored in a vector variable, ChangeNodeCoordinatesTo() can be used displace the node coordinates by these displacements\&.
.SH "Messages"
.PP
Due to the total garbage results that may arise, \fBMoveNodeCoordinatesBy()\fP will halt the simulation reporting a fatal error, if the target property is node a node or vector type property\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Model\fP< dim >::Out () const"
OutputMesh() will print to 'stdout' the mesh connectivity and boundary flags of each \fBNode\fP, IntegrationPoint, and \fBElement\fP\&.
.SH "Implementation"
